No Apple-shaped capabilities on Linux
====================================

Computer Use advertised eleven tools that only mean something on macOS — TCC permission prompts, the
Accessibility tree, Mission Control Spaces, the guided Teach overlay, `switch_display` — next to the
real ones. A tool that is offered and then fails teaches the model something false about the machine,
and it repeats that false thing in conversation; asked to look at the screen, it reached for an MCP
call because the prompt had told it Computer Use was an MCP server.

On Linux those eleven are not advertised at all. Screenshot, pointer, keyboard, scroll, clipboard,
windows and app launching stay: they are implemented against X11, the KDE portal and ydotool. The
prompt now states what this machine actually offers and describes MCP as third-party and optional,
never the first answer to a question about this machine.

